From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rzxvx <rrzxznewid@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:31:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mtd: apple-sftl: Give up a torn open block at load

A program that fails or is cut short can leave a torn page at the end of
an open block. The loader rejects any malformed record, so the whole
medium becomes unreadable: Cascadia met exactly that on 2026-11-16, when
a user-space tool reset the controller during a program. Its fix,
give_up_torn, skips such a page at mount and gives the superblock up
before the writer continues.

Port it:

- at load, accept one torn page per open block when it is the block's
  last programmed page: status good with metadata that is not a record,
  or erased status over programmed bytes, including a torn first page;
- let the journal miss exactly that page's four sequences, after the
  last record before it, and reserve them when nothing follows;
- before the first write, give every torn block up like a failed one,
  moving its live pages to the append point;
- a torn data page in a failed program is now given up too, keeping its
  four sequences used.

Read errors, refresh and unknown statuses still stop recovery, as does
a torn page followed by another programmed page and a torn summary.
Cascadia's walk skips any page that does not read with a good status;
here only malformed metadata counts, because #93 rejects unknown
statuses deliberately and what a cut-short PPN program reads back as
has not been observed.

Cascadia also lists the superblock as awaiting erase in its next
commit, so iOS does not program into it. The append writer writes no
checkpoint on the P105; the torn block stays an open block.

KUnit: the torn-metadata case now expects the data page to be given up
and reloaded; new cases cover a torn page left by something else, the
move at the first write, a second reload with the hole in the middle,
and a torn page followed by another. The relocation case that corrupted
the landing block's last page now corrupts the page before it, which is
still rejected.

Ported from Cascadia (https://github.com/rzxvx/Project-Cascadia) commits
7eaef71 ("a5: FTL: give up an SB with a torn page at writer start;
recovery module") and 131da0a ("a5: NAND: incident of 2026-11-16 and
the recovery procedure"), files sftl_mount.c (replay walk) and
sftl_write.c (give_up_torn), as of Cascadia ad28ba4.

Assisted-by: LLM
